Given, \[R_{a}=0.5\,\Omega,\qquad V_{t}=250\,\text{V},\qquad I_{a1}=20\,\text{A}\] \[N_{1}=1500\,\text{rpm}\] Since the field current remains unchanged, \[\phi \propto I_{f}\] Hence, flux is constant. Back EMF at \(1500\,\text{rpm}\): \[(E_{b})_{1500\text{rpm}}
=V_{t}-I_{a1}R_{a}
=250-20\times0.5
=240\,\text{V}\] Torque at \(1500\,\text{rpm}\): \[T_{1}
=\frac{E_{b}I_{a1}}{\omega}
=\frac{240\times20}{\left(\dfrac{2\pi}{60}\right)\times1500}
=30.56\,\text{N-m}\] Since flux is constant, \[T\propto \phi I_{a}\] \[\frac{T_{1}}{T_{2}}
=
\frac{I_{a1}}{I_{a2}}\] For \(I_{a2}=10\,\text{A}\), \[T_{2}
=
\left(\frac{I_{a2}}{I_{a1}}\right)T_{1}
=
\left(\frac{10}{20}\right)\times30.56
=
15.28\,\text{N-m}\] \[\boxed{15.28}\]

If supply voltage in a separately excited DC motor is constant, then input power is \[P = V_{t} I_{a}\] Since \(V_{t}\) is constant, \[P \propto I_{a}
\qquad \text{(1)}\] Torque developed is given by \[T = K\phi I_{a}\] For constant field voltage, field current remains constant and hence flux is constant. \[\phi=\text{constant}\] Therefore, \[T \propto I_{a}
\qquad \text{(2)}\] From (1) and (2), \[T \propto P\] Hence, the correct option is \[\boxed{B}\]

